A||||13||FieroSTETZ||car keeps overheating||||1||
Z||000000||FieroSTETZ||06-04-2000||09:28 PM||||my 87GT keeps overheating. after the first time it did it, i figured it musta been a freak accident- after the 2nd time, i took out the thermostat- third time, i have so far replaced my EGR valve (only code in the memory) i know that coolant is flowing to and from the radiator, i know that the fan comes on at high temps, i know the sender is good (when it hits red, coolant boils out) what could be wrong? i called a shop and they said i need new/reman. cylinder heads- can someone help me on this one?||||reg||1
Z||000001||GT Bastard||06-04-2000||09:47 PM||||Whoa.... Why did they say you needed reman cylinder heads?  That sounds a little outrageous.  Have you tried burping the system?  Does it happen ALL the time, or just every now and then?  Are you POSITIVE there are NO leaks anywhere?  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||CUTTER||06-05-2000||02:08 AM||||Does it overheat when just driving, sitting ect? it it does it only when your sitting for a few minutes i would check the fan. It could be that it is only comming on after it overheats. Thats what happend to me. Also check the thermostat.||||reg||1
Z||000003||FieroSTETZ||06-05-2000||02:35 AM||||well, there is no thermostat in there now- and i have burped the system a cuople times, just to make sure the air was all out. it overheats only intermittently. if i start the car and drive it for 10-15 minutes without the AC on, i goes right up past 220 fairly quickly. i sat the car on my driveway for 15 minutes idling, it got to a little more than halfway, then the fan started and it cooled off to about 1/2 to 3/4 of the gauge. if someone could help me out, i would be very appreciative- last time the car did it, i did not notice the temp gauge rise and the coolant boiled out onto my tires, and i almost crashed on the freeway. btw, shouldnt the car run pretty cool on the freeway? only recently, it has been running above half on the freeway. i am not driving hard, been shifting at 2500 to save gas- also figured that'd make less heat||||reg||4
Z||000004||CUTTER||06-05-2000||02:44 AM||||I dont know, wish i could help you out...i would just keep the AC on to keep the fan running....Thats what i do to keep it cooler since my AC doesnt work and I dont notice its on anyways. Take it and get it check out at a local shop. might cost a bit more but at least you will get it fixed and it will not boil on your tires and make you crash. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Sage||06-05-2000||07:14 AM||||When you burped it, did you jack up the front of the car and have it idling while you filled the radiator?  If not, you might try that.  Sometimes these cars can be down right cantankerous, when it comes to getting the air out! <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000006||sdm1964||06-05-2000||08:02 AM||||I had the same problem, I had the radiator rotted out and replaced the water pump. I put in that dexicool coolant, Car still seems to run hoter than it should. I noticed the SE that i bought has an air deflector under the nose that the GT doesn't. But look to see if the long coolant lines under the rocker panels have any kinks that might restrict flow. just my 2 cents||||reg||8
Z||000007||Ditz88GT||06-05-2000||11:17 AM||||I'm having the same problem.  I just replaced the head gaskets, and it still over heats.  Are you getting coolant back to the engine.  Mind pushes the coolant up to the radiator, and out the overflow tank.  But then none back to the engine.  I think I have a clogged pipe.  Thats what I'm checking anyway.  Maybe you should check the samething.  Good Luck!||||reg||7
Z||000008||Carrolles||06-05-2000||01:15 PM||||Look under the rocker panels to see if one of the coolant supply/return pipes may have gotten crushed from an improperly placed jack.<P>These are stainless steel pipes (about 1 inch diameter) that run from front to back just under the doors.||||reg||1
Z||000009||GT Bastard||06-05-2000||02:09 PM||||Get yourself a 180 degree thermostat and leave it in place!  Removing the thermostat won't necessarily make it run cooler. It can cause the coolant to rush through the radiator so fast that it doesn't get cooloed down enough.  Thats why race cars that don't run them sometimes use washers instead to slow the velocity of the coolant down. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||86SE||06-05-2000||02:27 PM||||I have a qustion, How do you burp the cooling system?<P>Thanks||||reg||5
Z||000011||Dan||06-05-2000||06:31 PM|||| Fill from the engine cap until full and go to the radiator and open untill coolant comes out and close.Resume filling from the rear engine cap .Be patient, it will keep goind down.Keep waiting and filling until it stops seeping dowm.Fill the overflow reservoir until full, start engine and run until it reaches about 210 and turn engine off. It will probably overheat again and blow air and water out the overflow.But as it cools down by itself,the radiator cap internally opens and gravity feeds coolant back into the radiator along with a cooling system vacuum created by boiling out the air with some coolant and virtue of the cooling system cooling back down.Just make sure your overflow reservoir doesn't empty when the engine cools back down or it will suck air back into the radiator. Although not related to the post, Fieros should be flushed regularly.  ||||reg||1
Z||000012||Dan||06-05-2000||06:35 PM|||| If you need new heads, you will probably find bubbles in the coolant. This comes from a warped head.Machine shop charged me $35 to surface the heads.If you have a milky colored oil, the coolant is going into the oil.||||reg||1
Z||000013||BAM-BAM||06-05-2000||07:14 PM||||  When burpping the coolant system, be sure to elevate the "REAR" of the vehical. That way your thermostat housing is the highest point in the system. Remove the radiator cap and fill till it oveflows the radiator, replace cap. continue to fill untill the thermo housing is full, let set a while and fill again, repeat as necessary, replace thermo cap. Start engine and allow it to reach normal operating temp (keep rear elevated), let engine cool and remove thermo cap, fill again. Now you should be able to drop the car and drive around. When you stop, let it cool and check the fluid level again, it might need more coolant. Do this every once in a while and you should'nt have any problems. This assumes that the coolant tubes under the vehical, the thermostat, the radiator, and water pump are all in working order. Now, the most over looked part of the system that can cause a host of problems. The CAPS; both the radiator and thermostat housing caps should be replace occasionaly.<BR>If there are any cracks in the cap seals the system looses pressure resulting in phantom heating. This can occur intermittantly or it can take the system down all at once. With these items you never know. Just a few thoughts and some advice that might help.<P> Late, BAM OUT!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">  ||||reg||12
